WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.149
Film Courage: In The Science of Screenwriting,
what’s your favorite chapter or maybe a

00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:04.990
chapter you felt was most challenging?

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:11.389
Dr. Connie Shears, Professor Crean at Chapman
University: I think my favorite chapter is

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:18.830
one because we got to give the overview of
the dance we were going to try to do for the

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:25.480
potential screenwriter or anyone really who
is interested in why movies are so fascinating.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:34.280
Why human beings will go into a dark place
with dozens of total strangers and sit quietly

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.280
for hours.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.750
I mean that behavior alone is worth a good
thorough psychological investigation.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:47.160
So in Chapter 1 I think we had a lot of fun
building up what we were going to do in the

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.160
rest of the book.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:57.739
But I like the chapter that is on the creative
stages of the screenwriter.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:06.351
That’s a chapter that involved quite a bit
of research in studying how the creative process

00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:12.530
itself plays out in any artist, in any creative
act.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:20.549
But in screenwriting, the importance of the
story itself.

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:24.740
So you had asked [Paul Joseph Gulino, the
co-writer of the book] a question about yes

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:31.390
or no, if you focus on the audience would
that make your screenplay a success.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.080
My answer would be a flat out no!

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.190
You must have the story.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:46.830
The story is what will make your screenplay
a success, the ability to pace your story,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:54.009
to weave your story, to tell your story in
a way that’s respectful, even playful with

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:57.900
the human brain that is going to be processing
it.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:04.039
That will give your story the attraction and
the further success.

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:09.090
But without the story, you can play people
all you want.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.600
If you don’t have something to tell them,
there is no reason to engage.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:20.610
And in the chapter on the creative processes
The Stages of Creativity, I got to do quite

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:27.900
a bit of research on what we know about creativity.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.280
Creativity is considered the ultimate cognitive
process.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.820
So we do all of these things in our brains,
right?

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:44.670
We pay attention, we perceive all of our different
sensory activities happen in the brain.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.790
So you don’t see with the brain.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.590
You see with your occipital lobe.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.030
You don’t hear with your ears.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.010
You hear with your auditory cortex.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:06.460
So all of these amazing things happen in the
brain but they happen in parallel processing

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:12.630
simultaneously so you’re hearing and seeing
while you’re thinking about what you’re

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.630
hearing and seeing.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:19.240
While you’re remembering what you’ve seen
and heard while you’re predicting what may

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.240
come.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:32.150
So our brains are these beautiful orchestrations
of simultaneous processes and creativity emerges

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:48.020
from that unique, totally individual, no two
brains do anything exactly alike so

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:57.310
what helps creativity, what hinders creativity
and what the screenwriter can use to get over

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.870
schlumps, you know?

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.420
Use to break through blocks.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:02.830
So that was my favorite I think.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.460
Film Courage: When thinking of your favorite
films are you able to look at it from another

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.490
perspective and see why…see the science
behind why you like certain films?

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.560
Dr. Shears: Sometimes…rarely.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.390
I’m a devotee of stories.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.710
I love going to movies and I don’t pretend
to be a scientist.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:23.230
I’m just a movie watcher in there.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:30.750
I can train myself to take (like Paul does)
take clips or sequences and then I can take

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:36.580
apart pieces of movies but then if I’m given
an opportunity to be told the story I take

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.940
it.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:43.460
Storytelling is the…it’s the human gift.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:54.030
There are (as far as we know) no other vertebrate
animals that engage in fantasy, that engage

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.070
in storytelling.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.010
It makes us uniquely human.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:09.310
And from my perspective it’s this connection
to language that as I was saying (has begun

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.030
before birth).

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:22.210
We listen in-utero for that voice that is
closest to us so the person carrying us is

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.720
the most important voice that we hear.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:33.520
And I think that it’s magic to me and I’m
a scientist, I’m all about data.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:45.750
But it is magic to me that we communicate
at all that we get each other even vaguely

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:50.920
and that we do it so effortlessly and so successfully.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:06:00.660
The gift of language, the gift of storytelling
is what I’ve taught for decades and it’s

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:10.620
just the magical, mystery tour and it’s
amazing how we don’t run out of language,

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:14.210
we don’t run out of stories.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:23.330
Boy meets girl or these kind of repetitive
plots but each in every movie about boy meets

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:27.960
girl captures us as an audience, holds us.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:34.590
We are as if we’ve never seen the whole
scenario before, that’s a good movie, right?

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:39.530
The ones that don’t make it are the ones
that have not added enough of that individual

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.590
creativity to the storytelling.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:47.370
Film Courage: So even language if it’s not
our native language we can still relate?

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:53.040
You know how people love seeing foreign films,
is there something different about this?

00:06:53.040 --> 00:07:02.110
Dr. Shears: That’s a great question and
I love talking about it as if you were deaf,

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:08.690
as if you did not have auditory processing
and so you were following along closed-caption,

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:11.340
you are reading the subtitles.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:19.220
You still get all of the emotion that is portrayed
visually but there is a lot of data that suggests

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:27.590
the sound not just the language but including
the language but the background music, the

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:35.750
tension sounds, the relaxing sounds, the auditory
cues that a screenwriter adds to their film

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:38.700
to create that emotional mood.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:48.680
When those are missing the movie is much more
of an intellectual process and much less of

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:53.210
a full-body emotional “I’m in this story.”

00:07:53.210 --> 00:08:00.449
So watching foreign films takes a different
mindset when you go to watch a foreign film

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:01.449
and you know you are going to be watching
the subtitles to get the dialogue.

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:07.419
If you are a hearing person and you know you
are going to be watching the subtitles to

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:08.970
get the dialogue.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:15.060
If you are hearing person and you can still
get the background cues to what is going on,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.790
it’s much more successful.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:26.270
For people who have auditory disabilities
movie watching is an entirely different experience.

